  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 223 PM Saturday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1... The chance of showers and storms will increase - \nfirst across western zones this afternoon-tonight, then throughout \ncntl NC on Sunday.\n\n\ufeffSouthwesterly flow aloft, and deep moisture characterized by PW \nvalues of 2-2.5 inches and 125-150% of normal, will be directed \nacross the Carolinas and southern Middle Atlantic ahead of a mid and \nupper-level trough that will pivot into the OH and lower MS Valleys \nthrough Sunday and then weaken while drifting into the TN Valley and \nsouthern Appalachians through early Mon. Several MCVs will probably \nbe embedded within that swly flow regime across the South Atlantic \nstates and may locally focus ascent along their track. \n\nAt the surface, moist, sly/sely flow will continue across the \nCarolinas, in a moist/warm sector downstream of a ~1003 mb low that \nwill drift across the OH Valley. A diffuse, outflow-reinforced \ndifferential heating zone, between initial waves of convection and \nlower and more-widespread overcast over wrn NC, may develop over the \nPiedmont through Sunday.\n\nAs forcing for ascent and increasing deep moisture edge ewd ahead of \nthe aforementioned trough aloft, with related lifting and removal of \na prominent temperature inversion around 700 mb on regional RAOBs \nthis morning, waves of diurnally-maximzed convection will develop \ninto initially the srn and wrn Piedmont this afternoon-tonight. That \nregime should then continue ewd and across cntl NC Sun-Sun night, \nprobably focused along MCVs and perhaps in a band along the \naforementioned surface boundary over the Piedmont. Deeply moist, \nuninhibited, and conditionally unstable profiles --weakly to \nmoderately so-- will otherwise favor the development of scattered to \nlocally numerous cells amid 25-30 kts of swly flow. A (Marginal) \nrisk of water-loaded, damaging downbursts will result. Although \nheavy downpours will also result, relatively fast cell motions to \nthe northeast will counter some tendency for training and \nconsequently limit the overall risk of flooding. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... Mainly diurnally driven showers and storms expected \nMonday through Friday.\n\nAloft, expect persistent deep swly flow into the area Mon and Tue as \ncentral NC sits on the eastern side of a trough/remnant s/w low, \nwith Bermuda high pressure to the east. The trough/low should \nweaken/lift newd by mid-week. While Bermuda high remains in place to \nthe east, another trough with embedded shortwaves will progress ewd \nacross the nrn CONUS, from the Plains to the Northeast, Wed through \nSat. How far south and east the trough extends will depend on the \nstrength and position of the Bermuda high. At the surface, still \nexpect a persistent sly component of the wind across much of the \narea, as no fropa is expected to occur, with continued advection of \nwarm, moist air into the area through the week. GFS forecast \nsoundings indicate PWATs around or above 2\" across much of the area \nthrough at least mid-week, highest Mon and Tue, with the best \ninstability (300-800 J/Kg) and shear (~20 kts) on Mon. Best chance \nfor showers and storms will be Monday, with the chance for generally \ndiurnally driven showers and storms to continue through the rest of \nthe week. All of central NC is in a Marginal Risk from the WPC for \nexcessive rainfall on Mon, with a continued risk across the east on \nTue.",
